00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:01.640
as they enter into this world?

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:03.613
- They need to get something

00:10:03.613 --> 00:10:06.160
that they feel comfortable
shooting first off.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.073
And it also depends on whether or not

00:10:08.073 --> 00:10:09.873
this is something that they're
gonna use for home defense

00:10:09.873 --> 00:10:12.280
or if they're gonna go get
their concealed carry license

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.480
and if it's gonna be something

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:14.523
that they carry.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.560
I have a lot of different firearms

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.650
for different purposes,

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.030
whether it's my bedside,

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:20.863
I hear something,

00:10:20.863 --> 00:10:22.420
my alarm goes off in
the middle of the night,

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.700
or if I'm wearing a cocktail dress

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.800
and I'm going to an evening event,

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.200
there's something else
that it's like shoes,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.180
you gotta have some variety.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.660
But I always say you
can't really ever go wrong

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.100
with a shotgun, you really can't.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:37.160
If you don't have a rifle,

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.340
if you've never shot a semi automatic,

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:41.460
if you don't have a handgun,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.450
shotguns, that's always like
a really good first choice.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:46.500
And the cool thing about gun owners

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.360
is that they are so incredibly helpful.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.550
Good law abiding gun owners
all across the country

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.780
are so eager to share with you

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:55.260
what they have found works for them

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.093
and what doesn't,

00:10:56.093 --> 00:10:57.500
what kind of training works really well.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.570
There's all kinds of, like
you have the California Rifle

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.080
and Pistol Association out where you are,

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.640
some really awesome people
that do really good training,

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.950
they're like up to date with everything.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:09.770
And they have really good
recommendations as well

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:10.670
as to where you can go,

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:11.770
what ranges you can go to.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.430
I mean, people even get it
down to ammo selections,

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:16.450
for crying out loud.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.690
So there's so many resources out

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:19.523
but I always tell people,

00:11:19.523 --> 00:11:22.770
if you've never been around firearms,

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:24.730
shotguns are always something
really good to start with.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.950
For handguns, I like to
recommend like a Glock 19,

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.270
which is a personal preference of mine,

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.190
Sig Sauer P365, M&amp;P.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.520
I mean, there's a lot of
good choices out there.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.690
And a lot of the
manufacturers out there, too,

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:40.180
these all started,

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.510
a lot of them are American companies,

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.500
American manufactured, American jobs,

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.690
and that's one of the
other focuses, I think,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.560
of our issues today

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.260
is keeping everything
right here in the U.S.A.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:51.100
- All right, Dana, thanks
for doing this pickup.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.770
And now I present everyone
else with my interview

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.448
with Dana Loesch.

00:11:55.448 --> 00:11:58.031
(upbeat music)

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.090
Hey, I'm Dave Rubin

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.260
and this is the Rubin Report,

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.530
quick reminder everybody to subscribe

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.320
to our YouTube channel and click

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.480
that little bell over there
so that you're notified

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.120
when our videos come out.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.360
And joining me today is a
wife, a mom, a radio host,

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:16.050
and author of the new
book "Grace Canceled,"

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:19.017
Dana Loesch, finally,
welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:12:19.017 --> 00:12:20.480
- Thank you for having me, I appreciate.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:21.410
It's good to be with you again.

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.120
This is like what, twice in now one month,

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:25.870
this is the most we've ever spoken.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.010
- We had gone years not speaking

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:32.410
or at least not in full
sentences, just through Twitter,

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:35.330
and then I was on your
show a couple days ago

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:38.850
and we booked you just as lockout began.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:42.490
You were gonna to come
to LA and then lockout.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.830
- I dropped the book right
at the start of a lockdown,

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.560
which is perfect timing.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.720
Publishers love that, they love that.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:51.810
- So you and I both did the same thing.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:53.950
We both released books in
the middle of a lockout.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.010
How's it going for you,

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:56.260
did they try to stop you?

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:58.630
My guys really wanted us to push

00:12:58.630 --> 00:12:59.463
but I was like,

00:12:59.463 --> 00:13:01.087
"No, we're getting this
freakin' thing out,

00:13:01.087 --> 00:13:02.590
"I can't sit on it anymore."

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.340
What was that process like for you?

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.090
- Well, it was a little daunting.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:09.860
I had the first week press tour,

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.170
which is always the craziest

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:12.700
when a book is out, that first week.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.420
And I was in New York for most of it.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.130
This was right at the end of February.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:19.360
And then I went to D.C.
when they were having CPAC

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:20.950
and I had an event there.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:23.620
And then I was to go
to Northern California,

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:25.530
and then it was to see you in LA,

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.658
and then do a couple of
other things in SoCal,

00:13:27.658 --> 00:13:28.810
and then I had to go to Minneapolis,

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:32.170
and there were 22 dates that
I had across the country.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.830
And one by one, they all
started getting canceled.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.220
And the first one was in San Francisco,

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.840
and then Sacramento followed,

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:40.687
and then my publisher was like,

00:13:40.687 --> 00:13:43.177
"I don't think that you
might have like difficulty

00:13:43.177 --> 00:13:45.757
"in getting home even if you
fly out there at this point.

00:13:45.757 --> 00:13:49.360
"So maybe we should start
canceling some stuff."

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.343
So yeah, it was just that first week.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:53.880
- Yeah, and here we are.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.210
All right, so we're gonna
do this on the Skype

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.440
or whatever pipes or wires we're using.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:13:59.690
I'm not even sure at the moment,

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:01.270
but we'll go ahead and do it anyway.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:04.510
I know that most of my audience
obviously knows who you are,

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.720
but I think for the
people that maybe don't,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:07.900
or maybe know your name,

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.160
but don't know exactly what you do,

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:10.993
I think they sort of,

00:14:10.993 --> 00:14:13.270
"Oh, Dana Loesch, that's the gun lady,"

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:15.220
like something like that.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:16.590
How would you really,

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:17.710
I mentioned mom and wife

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:20.310
and I took that stuff
from your Twitter bio,

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:21.540
but what do you really sort

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.090
of consider yourself first
at a professional level?

00:14:25.469 --> 00:14:26.510
- I think professionally,

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:30.060
I mean, just radio host, and
author, and TV commentator

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:33.880
and I have been in politics
for quite a long time.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:34.713
I actually got started

00:14:34.713 --> 00:14:37.640
in college doing opposition
research for Democrats.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:41.890
And so that's how professionally
politically it started.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:45.080
And I cover so many issues,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:46.630
but obviously the Second Amendment I think

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.940
is the one that I'm most known for,

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.960
just because it's such a personal issue.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:53.040
And it's something about
which I'm so passionate,

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.270
and I know a lot about it

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:57.150
and I shoot a lot and
I have a lot of guns.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.610
And so it just kind of goes hand in hand,

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:04.810
but I think most people
sort of see me in that vein.

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:06.300
And maybe like the devil incarnate.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.590
It just all depends, I think
on my political position.

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:10.860
- Well, speaking of the devil incarnate,

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:12.330
I am gonna read some of the blurbs

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.440
on the back of your book

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.280
because you did something
actually pretty hilarious

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.113
with the back blurbs,

00:15:17.113 --> 00:15:19.060
but we'll hold that for just a sec.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.700
So before we dive into guns,

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.300
and we'll talk about
all that hot topic stuff

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:23.633
and all that.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.650
What's going on with you and lockdown?

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:27.730
How's life been?

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.010
What are you feeling about
how the states are going

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.050
about doing things?

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.190
As we're recording this right now,

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:35.430
we are holding it for a little
bit, but I just found out

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.660
that it sounds like my state of California

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:39.830
or at least my adopted state of California

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:42.140
is now gonna be closed
for another three months,

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:44.680
even though virtually nobody is sick

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:46.920
and everyone's going bananas.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:48.070
How's it been for you?

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.553
What are you hearing from your
audience and the rest of it?

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:53.110
- For me, it hasn't changed a lot

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:55.410
because I'm kind of like
you, I work from home.

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.230
But I have gotten busier,
as weird as it is,

00:15:58.230 --> 00:15:59.830
because now my kids are home?

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.140
My oldest son's in college, and he's home,

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:03.370
which is actually pretty awesome

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:05.970
because he wants to go
into constitutional law.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.420
And so he has a lot of very
politically-charged classes.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.170
And I just look at it as
an excellent opportunity

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:13.320
to set straight anything

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.990
that he has unlearned while
he has been in college

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:16.970
and actually show him like

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:20.850
this is how you approached
balanced research.

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:22.460
And this is where you go

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.630
and you get a multitude
of different viewpoints,

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:26.685
and iron sharpens iron.

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:28.100
It's been a little hectic

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:29.020
because the kids are home.

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:34.020
But otherwise, I'm kind of a
situational extrovert anyway,

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:38.250
so on that, and it hasn't
really affected me.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.160
And I am really blessed in that regard.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.430
But what has affected me
is hearing so many stories

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:44.780
of people and friends of mine

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:45.960
who own businesses,

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.770
and they're terrified as
to what's gonna happen.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.100
And the town in which I live,

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:52.600
we've lost a number of businesses already.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:56.610
I was talking to another friend
of mine who owns a salon,

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.441
and she's terrified about
what's gonna happen.

00:16:59.441 --> 00:17:02.900
They can't get those
paycheck protection loans

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:05.160
because some of the banks
won't call them back,

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:06.240
they go with Chase Bank,

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.170
they're not hearing back from Chase Bank.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.560
And that's not like the exception,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:12.650
that seems to be sort of the general rule

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:13.670
with these situations.

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:15.840
It's really hard for
some of these businesses

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:18.900
to hear back from some of
these banks and to just get.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.300
When you consider that these
people have had their ability

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:23.683
to engage in commerce,

00:17:24.948 --> 00:17:27.770
it really is a quasi eminent domain.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.150
There's no other better
way I can describe it.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:33.900
And their ability to
generate income and to engage

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:35.550
in commerce has been seized.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:36.383
And they're told,

00:17:36.383 --> 00:17:40.050
"Well, we'll provide for
your needs temporarily,"

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:42.410
but then they're not
able to even access that.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:43.570
It's really rough.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:45.300
And I don't think that people,

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:47.140
you're talking about LA County,

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:51.130
maybe going under an additional
three months of lockdown.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.000
I don't think that that's gonna happen.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.340
They can't survive like
that, people cannot survive.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:18:00.400
- What do you make of
our public officials,

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:03.940
what I would basically
say is like an inability

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:06.390
to talk to us in a mature way,

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:09.450
like, even when I just heard
this three-month thing,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.750
which it sounds like it's
still we're not totally sure.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:13.700
But like they're floating out this idea.

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:16.230
It's like they don't tell
us why they're doing things.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.950
They don't say, oh, these things.

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:20.290
These policies have worked here.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:21.920
These policies haven't worked here.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.980
They don't say, oh, we
haven't found any cases

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:25.813
that are killing people

00:18:25.813 --> 00:18:28.560
and people under 40 that
are otherwise healthy,

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.060
but we know it's affecting people over 80.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.860
There's just no sense making coming out

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:35.760
of almost anyone at a political level.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.210
And I don't mean everybody,

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.490
I think Greg Abbott in Texas

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:40.810
is doing a pretty nice job.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:42.160
There are a couple people doing it.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.760
But that's so many of our
political leaders think

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:47.240
that just because they say something,

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.580
that that sort of is divine right

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:51.340
just then and there.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:53.080
- Yeah, and a lot of those orders

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:54.180
that you mentioned,

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.680
I mean, they're not law per
se as one thinks of law.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:00.570
I mean, they don't have
the force or effect

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.400
of law traditionally,

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.180
something passed by state legislatures

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.460
or something passed by Congress.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.870
And Newsom is weird,

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:08.570
your Governor's weird because he started

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.660
out kind of doing okay.

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:13.330
And then he lost his
senses and then he decided

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:14.670
to punish Orange County.

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:15.503
And you're right,

00:19:15.503 --> 00:19:18.550
there is a little bit of
nannying going on there

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.090
in terms of the way that they
speak to you about things.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.530
I feel like we're not kids,

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:23.580
we fund you.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.870
We pay your salaries,

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.520
we're responsible enough
to understand why it

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:28.840
is you're telling us these things,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.570
similar to in the very beginning,

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:31.610
when they were telling folks,

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:32.710
you don't need to wear masks.

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:34.500
Don't worry about wearing masks.

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:36.670
Definitely don't go and get those N95s

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:37.910
or any surgical masks.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:38.743
You guys don't need those.

00:19:38.743 --> 00:19:39.720
It's not gonna stop it.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:41.910
It turns out they were just
terrified that everybody

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.400
was gonna rush to buy those masks.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.510
And after that nobody wanted to believe

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:48.410
what any of these leaders
were telling us anymore,

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.178
because if they're gonna lie
about something like that,

00:19:50.178 --> 00:19:52.810
what else would they misconstrue?

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:53.660
- And not only that,

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:56.500
but if you were to put
a video out right now,

00:19:56.500 --> 00:19:58.750
if we told people not to wear masks,

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:00.860
our videos would be taken down on YouTube

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:04.240
even though the WHO was
saying that very same thing,

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:05.913
just a couple weeks ago.

00:20:06.965 --> 00:20:09.780
So I know we're like-minded
on the liberty side of things

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.630
and we believe in the constitution,

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.930
I mean real political radicals here.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:15.910
But for the liberty minded people

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:19.490
that do want to figure
out a way to put food

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:20.520
on their family's table,

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.690
that want to figure out a
way to go to work again,

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:26.170
or literally even I was
saying to David last night,

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:29.160
I would like to invite
friends over for dinner,

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.870
friends that I know
have been in quarantine,

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.390
they're healthy, like
not criminal activity,

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.320
but like we just have
friends over for dinner.

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:37.920
But what do you think the
liberty-minded people,

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:41.040
like what are the best tactics
that we should be doing

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.780
as we try to push on the government

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:44.920
as opposed to just like running around

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:47.540
and just masks off and holding like,

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.080
do you see a way we can do this sensibly

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:52.920
if the government is
not gonna do it for us

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:53.753
or something like that?

00:20:53.753 --> 00:20:55.644
- Yeah. No, I think
that's a good question.

00:20:55.644 --> 00:20:57.740
I love the story of Shelley Luther,

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:01.180
the salon that's maybe
like 45 minutes to me.

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:05.700
She really had reduced
the salon's capacity.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:08.090
She was following all of the guidelines

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.770
that all the medical
professionals were putting out.

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.660
She wasn't doing it as a stunt.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:14.130
But she legitimately was another one

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:14.980
of those business owners

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.700
where she couldn't go
and get any kind of loan,

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:17.660
any kind of help.

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:19.380
She has three kids, single mom,

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.470
she had a rent to pay for her business.

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.510
I mean, she really
needed to generate income

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:25.070
the best way she could.

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:28.600
I love the civil
disobedience aspect of it.

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:30.070
Here's one of the things
that really bugs me

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:34.330
about the mantra from
the house arrest people.

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:36.960
I can't even bring myself
to call it lockdown anymore.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:40.500
They think that it is that
the law mandating everyone

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.710
or what they profess to be law mandating

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:46.120
that everybody stay home is
just because it is a law,

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:47.920
or they perceive it to be a law.

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.330
And that it's not because it's right,

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:53.360
or because it's just,
or ethical, or moral.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.990
It derives all of its power from the fact

00:21:55.990 --> 00:22:00.400
that it is a law which seems
really ridiculously cyclical

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:02.690
and I don't really think

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:04.890
that they have a good
understanding of that.

00:22:05.811 --> 00:22:07.900
So I like the civil
disobedience aspect of it.

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:08.940
I was telling a friend of mine,

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.760
I am totally into the
speakeasy culture now.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:14.060
I'm all about let's do it speakeasy style

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:16.520
and I was even telling a friend of mine

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:17.950
who works with the governor here in Texas.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.527
I'm like, "I'm going to just do speakeasy,

00:22:19.527 --> 00:22:20.640
"y'all can come at me."

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:21.943
Come and make me instead of,

00:22:21.943 --> 00:22:23.770
come and take it, come and make me

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:25.930
and just have like speakeasy salon,

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:28.250
I haven't had a hair
trim in like 10 weeks.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.030
I know it's first-world problems.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.730
But just to kind of get
back to normal a little bit

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.830
and also give people a
way to earn some money.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:37.780
But the state has been,

00:22:37.780 --> 00:22:39.950
they've actually been kind of clever,

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.570
in that they were threatening
a lot of the hairdressers

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:46.490
and cosmetologist with the
removal of their licenses.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:48.090
So they do have to be careful

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.040
because you always have that.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.570
I call it that one turd in the punch bowl

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:52.990
that always ruins everything.

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:54.800
They always want to go
tell on the government,

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:58.860
someone is cutting hair against
the lockdown regulations,

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.170
we're gonna go tell the police,

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.820
we're gonna go tell the licensing office

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:04.490
and we're gonna have their license pulled.

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:05.323
It's ridiculous.

00:23:05.323 --> 00:23:07.920
And so I like the civil disobedience.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:12.370
But I jokingly say that the more

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:15.040
that the state pushes against the people.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:16.800
I'm not saying that's
how you get a boogaloo.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:18.820
But I'm saying that
might be how you get one

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:21.190
if you keep up those measures.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:22.730
- Yeah, well, speaking of shorter hair,

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:25.730
I'm not saying that my hair
is shorter than it was,

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:26.850
say three days ago,

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:31.240
but maybe it is, I'm just saying,

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:32.480
I'm not really saying anything,

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.288
but maybe it is, maybe it isn't.

00:23:35.288 --> 00:23:38.423
What do you think this
says about generally like,

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:41.750
for those of us that are
worried about authoritarianism,

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:44.750
for those of us that are worried
about governments run amok.

00:23:45.617 --> 00:23:49.020
So many people sort of do
take what you just said there,

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:52.490
that they hear a law and then
that in and of itself means

00:23:52.490 --> 00:23:54.400
it must be just or right or something,

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.020
and then they just sort of acquiesce.

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:57.970
Do you think that is almost like a symptom

00:23:57.970 --> 00:23:59.890
of the success of the West,

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:04.580
that we don't even know now
when tyranny is encroaching

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:06.620
on our lives like, okay, six months,

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:08.160
we're all gonna sit in
our house for six months,

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:09.630
40 million people will be unemployed,

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:11.230
the economy will be tanking.

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:12.940
And we're gonna all go, wait,

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:13.773
did anyone get sick?

00:24:13.773 --> 00:24:14.730
Did anyone get sick?

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:17.960
- Yeah, no, I think that's a great point.

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:19.640
And I think some of it is.

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.420
There are a lot of people in this country

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:24.500
in our generation who've never,

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.760
they've never experienced a dictatorship.

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.600
They haven't experienced
any kind of tyranny.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:33.543
A lot of us, obviously,
we lived through 911.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:38.240
But we've been really
far removed, thankfully,

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:40.270
from a lot of really bad stuff

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:42.420
that previous generations have endured.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:44.150
And so we don't really have anything

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:44.983
to compare it to.

00:24:44.983 --> 00:24:46.497
And I think for some, they think,

00:24:46.497 --> 00:24:47.960
"Oh, well, it's just temporary."

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:49.447
And isn't that kind of
how it always starts?

00:24:49.447 --> 00:24:50.847
"Oh, it's just temporary,

00:24:50.847 --> 00:24:52.840
"just a little bit of
our liberty for safety,"

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:53.960
which is a horrible trade off

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:57.030
and you never get a
return on your investment.

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:59.330
And with this, they think,
well, these are good people.

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:00.610
These are people we elected

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:03.010
that are telling us we need to stay home

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:04.210
and we need to listen to them.

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:05.740
And again, it goes back to just

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:07.670
because it's an executive order,

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:08.890
or it's a regulation

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:09.723
or it's a law.

00:25:09.723 --> 00:25:10.960
That doesn't mean it's just.

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:12.100
It doesn't mean it's right.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:14.020
And there are a lot of
conservatives, I have to say,

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:16.220
that are kind of going
along with this too,

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:18.610
which really surprises me.

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:19.950
Because these are people

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.020
who would complain against Red Flag Laws

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:25.230
and other forms of authoritarianism.

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:26.063
But with something like this,

00:25:26.063 --> 00:25:28.560
I mean, they don't even
question what the trade-off is.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:29.630
I mean, for instance,

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:30.740
what is flattening the curve?

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:31.810
Didn't we already flatten it?

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.560
So now we've gone from
flattening the curve

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:36.590
to we have to stay indoors
until there's a vaccine.

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:39.340
Well, no vaccine is ever 100% foolproof.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:41.383
So what is the ultimate point here?

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.200
- Yeah, there's so much here.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.190
That's actually an interesting
segue to your book,

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:48.780
because obviously you talk
a lot about outrage culture.

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:51.000
It's one of the things that
I talk about a lot too.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.440
And I think partly what it is, with say,

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:56.160
the conservatives that
seemingly are giving over a lot

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:59.160
of their individual rights
to the government right now.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:00.090
I think what it is

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:02.390
is that they're honestly
afraid of the outrage machine,

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.760
they're afraid to push a little bit

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:06.240
on the liberty side of things

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:07.710
or on the open up side,

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:09.750
because they don't want that mob

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:10.760
to come for them.

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:12.210
They don't want to be seen as,

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:14.520
oh, these, the way the media frames it,

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:17.010
that it's these fringe racist, right?

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.950
That was what CNN was saying about

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:21.470
the protesters in Michigan,

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:23.890
as if people were protesting for racism.

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:25.510
They wanted jobs, basically,

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:27.200
they wanted to live.

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:28.130
But I think that's what it is,

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.460
like you get sort of these
average conservatives

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:32.340
who I would agree on on a lot of things,

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:33.670
but then they're just like,

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:35.688
I don't want to push it

00:26:35.688 --> 00:26:37.710
because I don't want the
thing to come for me.

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:40.080
And that's rarely how it works, right?

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:42.283
That policy doesn't work for too long.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:45.350
- And liberty is not
something that's supposed

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:46.290
to be comfortable.

00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:47.560
And that's the other thing.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:49.750
I mean, it's not a guarantee of comfort.

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:51.490
And it's not a guarantee of happiness.

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:54.030
That doesn't mean that you're
gonna live a life completely

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:55.050
without any challenge

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:55.883
or any adversity.

00:26:55.883 --> 00:26:56.990
That's not what freedom is.

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:00.110
I mean, freedom is the
ability to enjoy the fruits

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:03.020
of your labor and also the
consequences of your decision

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:05.300
and engage in free association.

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:08.110
Look, people were totally happy

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:10.550
when we were initially
told what, two weeks,

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:11.740
we would just like for you all

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:13.160
to kind of lockdown a little bit

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:14.490
and help us flatten the curve

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:17.440
so we don't overwhelm
the medical resources

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:19.570
and have like another Italy on our hands.

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:22.320
Everybody was more than happy to do that.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:24.110
It wasn't because we were listening

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:25.810
to the government ordering us around.

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:27.660
It's because we generally care

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:30.010
about the lives and safety and wellbeing

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:31.820
of other people regardless

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:34.310
of what political identity they have.

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:36.880
And I mean, if you're gonna call yourself

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:38.520
a limited government person,

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.610
I think part of that
goes with understanding

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:42.620
that voluntary stewardship,

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:45.350
good stewardship goes
hand in hand with that.

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:47.530
And people didn't go out and protest

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:50.210
until you had some of these tiny tyrants,

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:52.310
I think as they've been
nicknamed across the country,

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:54.950
really exerting authority
that they didn't have

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:56.350
and they shouldn't have exerted

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:58.410
and really getting into the weeds with it.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:00.040
Like burning seeds and saying,

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:02.170
you could take a kayak out on a lake,

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:04.320
but you can't take a boat with a motor

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.840
because apparently gas and
motors make the RONA grow,

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:08.120
I don't know,

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:10.830
and instituting like curfews
and all of this stuff.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:12.990
And that's when people began
to rightfully question.

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:14.180
Okay, wait a minute,

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:15.260
we've been doing all of these,

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.093
what is the point?

00:28:16.093 --> 00:28:19.840
And then the two weeks went
from two weeks to a month,

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:21.460
and then two months,

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:24.060
people just want to be told the truth,

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:26.160
and they want to feel as
though their elected officials

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:27.620
are being forthright with them.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:31.040
And all of this showcase
something else entirely.

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.390
And so that's when people
started protesting.

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:35.850
And to point out, too, in Michigan,

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:37.780
you always have and as I understood it,

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:39.080
there were small group of people

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:42.080
who came out and decided
to be butt heads about it.

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:45.170
But that is not in any
way indicative of all

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:47.670
of the protests that have been
happening across the country,

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.180
even in California.

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:51.870
And for the media and others to focus

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:53.480
on that one incident as a way

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:55.720
to invalidate the other protest

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:57.723
is a really disingenuous way

00:28:57.723 --> 00:29:00.930
of evaluating the concerns of real people.

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:02.560
And it just forces that chasm

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:04.950
between the sides apart further.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:06.700
- Just to be clear, so the
incident you're referring to,

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:07.533
are you talking about all the guys

00:29:07.533 --> 00:29:10.230
that were doing the open
carry in the state capitol?

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:11.063
Is that what you're
referring to specifically?

00:29:11.063 --> 00:29:13.010
- They had so many show up

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:14.300
and they had like some signs

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:15.930
and then there was one that was a plant

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:18.950
where they had like,
what was it a Nazi flag,

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:21.298
and it turned out, it
ended up being a plant,

00:29:21.298 --> 00:29:23.640
it was incorrectly reported or whatever.

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:28.640
I mean, all of that, the
theatrics and the messaging

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:31.930
and everything aside,

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:33.960
the fact that people
were were focusing more

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:37.230
on the protesters instead of
here you have elected officials

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:38.470
that are doing certain things

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:39.430
that they have no authority

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:41.280
in their state constitutions to do.

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:43.150
And they have the press,

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:44.870
which is supposed to be
the watchdog of the people,

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:47.200
not adequately or accurately reporting

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:49.640
on things for the people they
claim to care about so much.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:51.370
That's a little frightening,

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:53.080
which is why I'm thankful
for citizen journalism,

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:57.150
but that was frightening
and that's a problem

00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:00.420
and I think it just continues
to feed this distrust

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:03.370
that people have of authority, ultimately.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:05.820
- Yeah, that's a good
segue also for something

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:08.880
that's in the book that
you are always hitting,

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:11.260
very much in line with
what I'm hitting on.

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:14.603
Let's talk about the press a
little bit, sort of mainstream.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.210
Everyone's watching it collapse,

00:30:17.210 --> 00:30:19.330
everyone's watching the quote, unquote,

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:22.020
journalist destroy journalism.

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.990
Does this thing just need to flame out?

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:27.280
If you would have asked
me maybe six months ago,

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:28.440
I was still on,

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:31.300
oh, this thing can be fixed wagon,

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:34.490
or at least that we should
still be trying to fix it.

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:36.160
And now after Corona,

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.030
I'm just like,

